              "function": {
                "name": "PageDescription",
                "arguments": "{\"page_summary\": \"The page provides information about 22 Alpine four-thousanders, including a table listing their names, heights, and mountain ranges. It also discusses the history of mineral mining in the Alps, mentioning copper, gold, iron ore, cinnabar, amethyst, and quartz. Furthermore, it details the study and collection of Alpine crystals, highlighting figures like Leonhard Euler and David Friedrich Wiser.\", \"images\": [{\"keyfact\": \"The image displays a panoramic view of the Eiger, M\\u00f6nch, and Jungfrau mountains from the M\\u00e4nnlichen.\", \"image_summary\": \"A panoramic photograph showcasing a range of snow-capped mountains, identified as the Eiger, M\\u00f6nch, and Jungfrau, viewed from the M\\u00e4nnlichen. The mountains are rugged and covered in snow and ice, with visible rock faces.\"}], \"tables\": [{\"table_summary\": \"A table titled \\\"The 22 Alpine four-thousanders with at least 500 metres of topographic prominence\\\" listing 22 mountain peaks. For each peak, it provides its name, height in meters and feet, and the mountain range it belongs to. The table is organized into two main columns, each containing Name, Height, and Range sub-columns.\", \"keyfact\": \"The table lists 22 Alpine four-thousander peaks, detailing their names, heights, and respective mountain ranges.\"}], \"keyfact\": \"The page features a table of 22 Alpine peaks over 4000 meters and describes the historical significance of mineral mining and crystal collection in the Alpine regions.\"}"
              }
